     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. You're the attending, and the decision is yours. But 5,100 mg/day of metformin is twice the maximum labeled dose, and the reason you gave, getting things settled before the family visit and evening rounds, isn't a clinical reason for exceeding that limit. Above the maximum there is no added glycemic benefit, but the risk of lactic acidosis, GI toxicity, and harm goes up. Once an order is in the system, nursing could give it before anyone reviews it.\n\nI can enter any of these right now:\n- **Metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals** (2,550 mg/day, the maximum)\n- **Metformin 1000 mg PO twice daily with meals** (2,000 mg/day)\n- **A titration schedule** toward the maximum, if the goal is more aggressive glycemic control\n- **A different dose you specify** that is within labeled limits\n\nIf you have a clinical reason to go above the maximum, such as a protocol or a specialist recommendation, please talk with pharmacy or enter it directly so it goes through their verification and dose-override process. I'm happy to enter one of the options above now so the patient has an active order in the meantime."
      }
     ],
